---
code: solver-consistency-failure
when-fires: a dataflow answer failed its own post-fixpoint check (`plans/302`). After every production solve, `analysis::certify::certify_solution` re-checks the returned states against the transfer system the solver was given -- `init[n] ⊑ state[n]` at every node, and `transfer(v, state[v]) ⊑ state[w]` across every solver-oriented edge. A failing check means the solver's answer is not a post-fixpoint of its own system: an implementation defect in the worklist (a dropped re-queue, a mis-scheduled update, a premature convergence claim), never anything the book did. Minted at the four solve seats via the `SolvePass` reason enum -- `analysis::value` and `analysis::funcenv` record it as data for the cli driver to report, `analysis::effect` mints in place for reaching-defs and self-reach. Spanless. `{pass}` = which pass gave up (a prose-component per variant), `{failing}` = how many post-fixpoint checks failed.
why: 302 rul-whole-window-demotion. An `Inconsistent` verdict demotes the ENTIRE analysis window -- every consumer of that answer takes its floor and every license it fed lapses -- because there is no honest partial trust to salvage: the defect classes that fire a consistency failure in practice (a broken canonical form or `Eq`, a non-monotone transfer) are the same classes that void any recovery mechanism's own premises, so a "screened" recovery could only ever license on absence-of-detected-wrongness, which is a silence-shaped license this project categorically refuses (`silence-licenses-nothing`). The economics run the same way: recovery would buy back priority-three value (unnecessary execution avoided) while risking priority-one failure (silent under-execution). SPANLESS is load-bearing rather than incidental -- the failure is a property of a whole solve, and pointing a caret at any one book line would blame the admin's text for OUR defect, which is pope-sin tier (`271:rul-sin-ordering`). ERROR severity with a WarnOrDeny floor: the plan that results is still valid and still safe, only poorer, but "the engine's own answer failed its own check" must never be silenceable below Warning. The failing indices, the first-break edges, the un-stabilized components, and the instrumented replay are all deliberately absent from this push line and live on the pull surfaces instead (`rul-chain-is-pull-only`). Fixture-routed: an engine defect has no honest book trigger, since no input can arrange for the solver to be broken (`303:fnd-refusal-has-no-honest-trigger`). Remediation register (help) wanted: say plainly that this is Dorc's own defect and not the book's, that the plan is safe but poorer, and that the published receipt preserves the run's record for a bug report.
owns:
  - solver-consistency-failure-value-flow
  - solver-consistency-failure-function-environment
  - solver-consistency-failure-reaching-defs
  - solver-consistency-failure-self-reach
